// The Lost Centuries, chapter 3 (interlude session A). 537 AD: the second waking, in the Senate of Blood's new city, on the eve of the dedication of the Great Church.
// Reads the wake report from lc02's sleep (sack_to_byzantium), lc.seat, lc.court and lc.watchers from before. A night phase of two actions out of three places, then the day of the dedication.
// Ends with rest() and sleep("byzantium_to_plague"), the last segment of the chain, then => next, which ends Book One. The chapter's own Velati journal entry is content/journal/lc-537.tsb.
@chapter lc03
@title The Second Rome
@book burning-city
@version 3

# start
## Up again
$ lc.answer = "bargained"
$ lc.sunwalk = "refused"
$ lc.hill_537 = "none"
$ lc.court_537 = "wary"
$ lc.mason = "unmet"
$ _fed = 0
$ _pick = 0
~> waking
?if must_feed()
  ~> hunger
?end
---
~> writ
---
~> voyage
---
$ do phase("night", 2)
Constantinople does not so much welcome you as absorb you. There is a quay, and a customs house, and a very large number of people, and a smell of fish and incense and hot bread, and you are standing in the middle of it in a plain cloak and a perfectly good pair of boots, and nobody notices you at all. It is, on the whole, a great relief.
It is the twenty-fifth of December, in the eleventh year of the reign of the Emperor Justinian, and the Great Church is to be dedicated in two days. The city has been talking of nothing else since the autumn. You have, by the court's reckoning, two nights, and a morning.
-> night

# waking
?if wake("outcome") == "woken_early"
  ?if wake("by") == "hunters"
    It is a torch, and a crowbar, and a voice in a Latin that you have to listen to twice. There are four of them, soldiers, deserters by the look, of one army or another, with sacks. They have heard that the cellar on the hill holds the treasure of a Gothic king. They have found a cot, and a lamp, and a body that opens its eyes.
    The youngest has the sense to drop his torch. You are so hungry that the room has edges.
    $ _pick = 1
  ?elif wake("by") == "government"
    There is a smell of cold lamp oil, and a cough, and two Lictors, standing very stiff against the wall in the grey Senate cloaks, with a roll between them. They are young. They are, you think, quite as frightened of you as you would wish. They have been told to wake you with the greatest courtesy, and have not been told how.
    "The Senate sends its compliments," says the taller, in a thin voice, "and requires your presence." He swallows. "It said to say *requires*."
  ?else
    Someone has woken you, and it is not at all the person that you would have chosen. It is a hand on your shoulder, shaking, a voice saying your title, a smell of fear and good wine. They have a paper, and a reason, and a sort of desperate cheerfulness, and the thing that you notice, before you are properly awake, is that they are standing very close to the door.
    You are very hungry. You do not at all like the look of them.
  ?end
?elif wake("steward") == "prisca"
  Prisca is sitting on a stool at the end of the bed with a lamp, and her abacus in her lap, and a sheet of the Senate's vellum, and the look of a woman who has been waiting for you to wake for some time and has been doing a sum about it.
  "I came by sea," she says. "It took thirty-one days. I do not recommend it." She holds out the vellum. "It is from the court. I thought that you ought to read it before I did anything stupid."
?elif wake("steward") == "hanno"
  Hanno has put a lamp on the floor, and a tablet on your chest, which is a thing that he would not ordinarily do. "It came from Constantinople," he says. "It is under the seal of the Chamberlain. I have read it three times. It is a very courteous letter." He seems to be a good deal paler than usual. "I have found that I do not trust courteous letters."
?elif wake("steward") == "tiro"
  "I did not open it," says Tiro. He is sitting on the end of the cot, holding a sealed roll in both hands, and looking at it as a man looks at a snake that has been put in his lap. "I wanted to. I am a person who opens letters. But it had a seal on it that I recognized from a story, and I thought that you had better do it."
?elif wake("steward") == "keepers"
  The man holding the lamp is old. He is Valerian, he says, of the Valerii, a long way down the line from Aelia. He has her chin and Felix's patience. "It came by a Greek ship's captain," he says. "He would give it to nobody but the head of the house. He stood at the gate for two days. He is still there, I think, eating." He gives it to you with both hands. It is a roll of the finest vellum, with a seal of black wax and a small plain sign in the Senate's mark.
?else
  There is a clerk, with a lamp, a ledger and a packet. "A courier arrived from the East," he says. "He would deliver it to nobody but the principal. I have taken the liberty of waking you." He does not seem at all sure about the liberty.
?end
?if wake("place") == "collesanto"
  You are in the cellar on the hill. There is a wine rack along the wall again, and the floor is as hard as you remember, and somewhere above you, quite faintly, there is the sound of a bell.
?elif wake("place") == "haven"
  You are in the haven, in the city. It has been repapered, and a very large wardrobe pushed across the door. There is a plain wooden cross over the bed, put there by somebody who did not know what was behind the wardrobe and wished to be on the safe side.
?else
  The tomb in the rock has been opened. There is a lamp on the ledge, and a stone rolled aside, and a rim of grey winter light, that you do not at all mind, round the lip of the door.
?end
?if wake("outcome") == "woken_early"
  Your accounts, you gather, have not been looked after as they should. It is a thing that you will have to put right.
?end
<~

# hunger
## The first thing
* Find something warm and four legged
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is not what the hunger wanted. It is a goat, again. They are a remarkably consistent animal.
* Ask for a willing arm ?if wake("by") != "hunters"
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-2)
  It is a thing that has been arranged. It is quick, and it is clean, and you are very much ashamed of how well it works.
* Go after the one who ran ?if _pick == 1
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ cunning_brutal += 3
  You catch him on the stair. He does not scream, and you do not afterwards remember having decided. He is alive at the end of it. It is not at all to your credit.
* Go without
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a bad choice and you will pay for it before dawn.
<~

# writ
## The summons
It is a good vellum. It is written in a Latin that has been polished by a number of clerks until it has no edge on it whatever, and it says, in the end, a very simple thing.
?if lc.seat == "taken"
  *To a member of the roll of the Senate, greeting. The Senate sits at Constantinople, in the cistern of the Basilica, and requires the attendance of its member at the dedication of the Great Church on the feast of St Stephen. The matter touches the member's particular gift. It is to be answered in person.* It is sealed with the black wax and signed, at the foot, in a small clear hand, *Eudoxia, for the Chamberlain.*
?elif lc.seat == "declined"
  *To the man of the hill, greeting, and with the continued regard of the Senate. The Senate sits at Constantinople, in the cistern of the Basilica, and would take it kindly if you were to attend the dedication of the Great Church on the feast of St Stephen. The matter touches your particular gift. The Senate trusts that you will not find it necessary to decline twice.* It is sealed with the black wax and signed, at the foot, in a small clear hand, *Eudoxia, for the Chamberlain.*
?else
  *To the man called by the name of the hill. The Senate sits at Constantinople, in the cistern of the Basilica, and requires you at the dedication of the Great Church on the feast of St Stephen. You will present yourself to the Chamberlain. It is the last courtesy of the court.* It is sealed with the black wax and signed, at the foot, in a small clear hand, *Eudoxia, for the Chamberlain.*
?end
The Senate is, you read, at Constantinople. It has been there for a hundred and twenty-seven years, or the better part of the time. It has not been a thing that you have had to think of. You think of it now.
?if lc.court == "refused"
  You think, in particular, of a quarry, and a plain grey cloak, and a man who said that he would remember that you had been asked.
?end
The letter is not long. It does not say what the court wants. It is, in the way of such things, an invitation that you cannot decline, in a hand that has a great deal of experience of being refused.
<~

# voyage
## The long road east
It is a thing that you do not quite follow, afterwards. It takes the better part of a month. You go south by night, round the edges of two armies, to Brundisium, where the Gothic war has not reached, and take ship, in a big round merchantman, with a cargo of oil and a captain who has been paid a very large sum not to look at his passenger. You are in the hold by day, in the dark, with the oil, as a thing to be moved. At night you come up and stand in the bow, in the cold, with the sea going by under the stars like a great dark animal that has no idea that you are there.
You see Corinth, and a lot of islands. You do not see anything at all that you recognize. There is a thing about the sea at night that is a good deal like the sleep, and you do not care for it.
It is a Greek world. It is the thing that you notice. The sailors talk Greek, and the cook, and the captain, and the passengers, and the little boys on the quay. Latin is the language of the law, and the army, and the clerks. It is, you gather, the language that people use when they do not want to be understood by the people they are talking to. You find that your own is a little out of date. You have, according to the first mate, the accent of a statue.
<~

# night
## The city
:night
?if actions_left() == 2
  It is dark, and the lamps are lit all down the long colonnade of the Mese, and the city is a great busy glitter of oil and wax and polished marble under a sky as thick as black wool. It is all new. It is all so new that you can smell the plaster.
?else
  It is very late. The long streets are almost empty, and the wind is coming up off the Bosphorus with the smell of snow in it. The great dome of the new church is a darker shape against the dark, and there are scaffolds all about it, and a single yellow light, high up, where somebody is still working.
?end
* Go down into the cistern, where the court is waiting [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> cistern
  $ do act()
* Go to the Collis house, where the Italian letters are [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> house
  $ do act()
* Go and look at the Great Church, in the last of the night [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> church
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dedication
?end
-> :night

# cistern
## The court in the cistern
$ do discover("the_cistern_court")
$ do meet("eudoxia")
The door is in the cellar of a perfectly ordinary house, behind a perfectly ordinary wine rack, at the bottom of a perfectly ordinary stair, and there are a hundred and fifty-two more steps after that, and then there is the water.
It is the biggest room that you have ever been in. It is a forest of columns, in rows, in the dark, standing up out of black water, three hundred and thirty-six of them by the count of the boy who carries your lamp, going away on every side beyond the reach of the light. The ceiling is a series of low domes. The air is cold and wet and smells of stone. There is a very faint sound of falling water, everywhere, like a great quiet breath.
It is, you understand, a reservoir. It is the city's, built for the Emperor's palace, and it holds enough to withstand any siege. It is also, as of some years ago, the most secure room in the world. There are two columns at the far end that stand on carved heads, a woman with snakes for hair, one on its side, one upside down, and nobody will tell you why.
There is a platform of planks over the water, with lamps, and chairs. There are about forty chairs, and in each of them is a person you know, or ought to, in the way that you know the people of a house that you once lived in. The old faces, the Senate of Blood, as it was in Rome, in the dark, in a very large wet room. They have kept the chairs. They have kept the manners. They have, you see, kept the quiet.
Tarrax stands at the right hand, in the grey cloak, as he has always stood. Galerius is beside him with his tablet. In the middle, in a high-backed chair of black wood, with a rug on her knees, is a small round woman of perhaps fifty, with her hair in a plain net and the face of somebody's aunt.
"You are very welcome," says Eudoxia. She has a quiet, pleasant Greek voice, and a Latin that is better than yours. "I am so sorry about the stairs."
?if lc.seat == "taken"
  "Our member," she adds, and a stir goes round the platform, a thing like the shifting of leaves.
?elif lc.seat == "declined"
  "Our guest," she adds, and nobody stirs at all.
?else
  She does not say what you are. It is quite plainly a thing that she has decided not to.
?end
She has a number of questions. She asks them with the gentleness of a woman who has asked a great many, over a great many centuries, of people who were afraid to answer. What are you? Who made you? How long have you walked in the sun? Has it cost you? Do you know that there are some at this table who would have been very glad if you had not come? Do you know that there are some who would be sorry?
Nobody answers the last. You look at Tarrax. He looks at the water.
"We are not a cruel court," says Eudoxia. "We are a very old one. It is a thing that is, I am afraid, harder to forgive." She folds her hands. "Tell us what you think we ought to do about you."
* "I want nothing from you but to be left alone. I will give what I can."
  $ lc.answer = "humble"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 2
  It is the plain truth, and you say it plainly. There is a stir. Eudoxia nods slowly. "That," she says, "is not at all what we expected, and is a thing that I shall remember." Tarrax's mouth twitches. It is not a smile. It is the nearest thing to one that you have seen in four hundred years.
* "Name your price. I have a good deal more of the one than the other, and so, I think, have you."
  $ lc.answer = "bargained"
  $ do earn(-8)
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing += 1
  Eudoxia's eyebrows go up. It is a small thing. "Ah," she says. She looks at the platform, and the platform, to your surprise, almost laughs. "It is a great deal more sensible than the last gentleman." The terms are written down in an hour. They are heavy. They are, you think, fair. There is a tithe, and a seat at the table when it is wanted, and a promise, in a small clear hand, that the hill will be left in peace.
* "I walked in the sun before most of this table was born. I do not recall asking leave."
  $ lc.answer = "defiant"
  $ cunning_brutal += 3
  $ standing -= 1
  There is a silence you could cut with a knife. It goes on. Somewhere, a long way off, there is a drip. Galerius has stopped writing. Tarrax has turned his head. Eudoxia is quite perfectly still, with her hands folded on the rug, and a very faint line between her brows.
  "That is true," she says, at last, "as far as it goes." She does not raise her voice. "I should be glad if you would consider, in the course of the evening, how far that is."
~> cistern_end
<~

# cistern_end
She tells you what she wants. It is a small thing, and it sounds like nothing, and it is perfectly reasonable.
The Great Church is to be dedicated at noon on the feast, by the Emperor and the Patriarch, before the whole of the court and the greater part of the city. It will be the single most important event of the year, in the single most important building in the world, and the Senate of Blood, which has a hand in everything that the Emperor does, cannot send one person. It is noon. It is the most open hour of the day. There is no vampire in the city who can set foot in the light.
"Except one," says Eudoxia.
She wants him to be there. She wants him to walk across the forecourt, and in at the great door, in the daylight, in front of the whole city, and stand in the nave, and look at the Emperor's court, and come back and say what he saw. It is a very little thing. It would be, she says, a service. It would also, she does not say, be a thing that her people could watch from the shuttered litters that she will have at the western door, through a crack, at the cost of a very great deal of nerve. It would be a proof.
?if lc.answer == "defiant"
  Tarrax says nothing. He has not said anything. It is a thing that you do not at all like.
?end
You are not obliged to say, tonight, what you will do. The dawn, as Eudoxia points out, is a good way off.
<~

# house
## The Collis house
The Collis house at Constantinople is a narrow building on a side street near the harbour of Theodosius, with a good door, a small shrine to the Mother of God in the vestibule, and an office at the back full of the smell of wax. It is a good deal smaller than the one in Rome, and a great deal more orderly. It has a Greek factor, an elderly man called Nikephoros, who has been told, for as long as he can remember, to expect a letter, and has been quite sure that it would never come.
He has one for you. It has come by sea, in the winter, in a leather case, and has the plain rough seal of the keepers of the hill.
It is not from Valeria. She is a hundred and twenty years gone. It is from the head of the family now, an old man, who writes in a very bad Latin and a very careful hand. The Goths are at Rome again. It is a different lot, and a different king, and there is a great army of the Emperor's inside the walls, under a clever general, and the city has been besieged since the spring. The hill has sent what grain it can. The young men are on the wall. They are all quite well. It is, he says, a thing that the family has been through before.
There is a postscript. *We have built a chapel. The bishop's man said that it was a martyr's. We did not correct him. The people call the one in the cellar the Sleeper, and pray to him on the first of the month. It is a harmless thing. I thought that you would wish to know.*
You read it twice. It is not clear to you whether it is the funniest or the saddest thing that you have ever read. It is, you think, probably both.
There is a thing that you can do. It is not a large thing, from this distance. It is the only thing there is.
$ _mp_n = 0
$ _mp_rank = 0
:mp_more_1
* Send what you can in coin, and a draft for grain [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 1
    $ lc.hill_537 = "sent"
    $ _mp_rank = 1
  ?end
  $ do earn(-6)
  $ do send_home(6)
  $ humanity += 1
  Nikephoros writes the draft. It is a good one, on a good house, and will be honoured, if the war permits, by a Roman banker in Ravenna. It is the first time in your life that you have sent a letter by sea, and you find that you are standing at the door of the room, afterwards, with your hand on the jamb, for rather longer than the thing deserves.
* Send men: paid guards from the quays, with a captain who has served under the Emperor's general [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ lc.hill_537 = "guarded"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ do earn(-10)
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It is a bigger commitment than a draft. Nikephoros knows a man. The man knows a captain, a lean quiet Isaurian with an old wound, who has done this before, and thinks the price is fair. They sail in the morning, twelve of them, with the winter wind behind them, and a letter, and a good deal of private doubt. You do not ask about the war. You do not want to know.
* There is nothing to be done from here. Keep the letter ?if _mp_n == 0
  $ lc.hill_537 = "ignored"
  $ do village_harm(5)
  $ humanity -= 1
  It is, you tell yourself, perfectly true. It is a thing that nobody could do anything about. You fold the letter, very small, and put it in your sleeve. You carry it with you for a good many days, and you do not look at it, and you do not forget what it said.
