# church
## The Great Church
$ do discover("the_great_church")
You go up the long slope from the harbour, past the shut shops and the lamps, to the great open square before the church, and stand at the foot of it, in the dark, in the cold, and look.
It is, you think, the largest thing that has ever been built. It is not at all like the temples of Rome. It has no columns, no pediment, no front. It is a big brown mass of brick, in the dark, with a dome on it, and buttresses, and half-domes, going up into the sky like a hill that someone has taught to think. There are scaffolds all about it, and the ladders, and the barrows, and the heaps of marble waste, and from the very top of the dome, a hundred and eighty feet up, a single yellow light.
There is a man at the foot of the ladder. He is sitting on an upturned barrow, with a flask, and a rag, and a cut across the back of his left hand that he is attending to without any great urgency. He is forty, or so, with plaster in his beard, and the loose shoulders of one who has been doing the same thing for five years.
His name is Ioannes. He is a mason. He is, he says, the last on the dome, because the foreman has promised a bonus, and because the lamp up there is the one that he lit, and he'll be damned if he'll leave it. He does not at all want to talk. He does talk. He is a man who has been up a ladder for a great many hours with nobody to tell.
$ do meet("ioannes")
He has, he says, laid gold on the dome. It is a thing that nobody has ever done before. They have used glass, small squares of it, with gold leaf between two layers, tilted at an angle so that the light will strike it in the morning. He shows you one that he has kept, a tiny square of glass and gold, the size of a thumbnail, in the palm of his bleeding hand.
His heartbeat is the loudest thing in the square. You are quite surprised by how much you want to hear it.
* Hold the flask, and listen to him go on
  $ lc.mason = "helped"
  $ humanity += 2
  $ bond.ioannes += 2
  You listen to him for an hour, on a barrow, in the snow, in the dark, while he tells you about his wife, and his bad back, and the foreman, and the Emperor, and the cost of olive oil, and what it is to put the last of five years of your life into a thing that you will never see finished. He says that it is going to be a good building. He says that it will stand for a thousand years. He is perfectly right, as it happens. He is, in the end, rather drunk, and rather sentimental, and he presses the tessera into your hand, with the gold side up, and says that it is for luck.
  $ do obtain("great_church_tessera")
* Bind up his hand, and send him home
  $ lc.mason = "helped"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ bond.ioannes += 1
  It is a clean cut. He does not need it. You tear a strip from the hem of your cloak and bind it up, and tell him to go home to his wife, and he says, "You sound like her." He gets down off the barrow. He says that it is a funny thing, he has been lifting for five years and a stranger tells him to rest. He goes. He has, in the end, left the tessera on the barrow, and you pick it up, and put it in your sleeve.
  $ do obtain("great_church_tessera")
* Let him be. It is a good night for a man to be on his own
  $ lc.mason = "left"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You go on. It is a perfectly proper thing to have done. You think of him, afterwards, now and then, in the cold, with his cut hand and his flask and his bit of gold, and of the heart that you did not stay to hear.
~> church_end
<~

# church_end
The light at the top of the dome goes out. It is a very long way up. You watch it for a minute. It does not come on again.
<~

# dedication
## The feast of St Stephen
It is noon, and it is the brightest day of the winter, and you are standing at the foot of the steps of the Great Church in the middle of fifty thousand people, with the sun on your face.
It is a very good sun. It is the clean hard low sun of an Eastern winter, a white thin light with a bite in it, and it comes down over the roofs of the palace and the Hippodrome and the long white colonnades and strikes the great brown mass of the church in a way that does not at all make it beautiful. It is not a beautiful building from the outside. It is the other thing: it is big.
Nobody remarks on you. The city, in its best clothes, is going up the steps in a long slow tide, the court, the senators, the guilds, the beggars, the priests, a hundred thousand candles' worth of silks, and you go with it, in a plain cloak, with your head up. There is a long line of closed litters drawn up along the western wall, with thick curtains, and men with staves, and a number of very careful mortals standing at the carriers' heads who are watching you.
~> proof
-> inside

# proof
Somebody has to decide whether to be seen.
* Walk in, in the open light, past the litters
  $ lc.sunwalk = "shown"
  $ standing += 2
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ velati_regard -= 1
  You do not hurry. You go up the long stair in the full light of noon, in front of the shuttered litters, one step at a time, and you feel, quite clearly, from behind the curtains, from the thin dark slits where there are eyes, the entire weight of the Senate of Blood looking at a thing that it has been afraid of for centuries. You do not turn. You do not hurry. You get to the top, and into the vestibule, and through the great door, and you hear, behind you, very faint, from one of the litters, the sound of someone letting out a breath.
* Go round to the side, and slip in with the guilds
  $ lc.sunwalk = "refused"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You do not do it. You go round by the side door, with the guild of the silversmiths, who have their own banner, and a drum, and a number of very bad hats, and you let them go in front of you, and you come in with the crowd, in a corner of the nave, out of sight, and the litters at the western door see nothing at all but a crowd. It is a perfectly sound thing to have done. It will be remembered.
<~

# inside
## The nave
You come in under the great vault, and you stop.
It is a thing that cannot be described, and many people have tried. It is not a building at all. It is a space. It is a thing that has been hollowed out of the air, and lined with marble, and gilded, and hung with lamps, and roofed with a dome so high, and so wide, and so light, that it seems to float. It does not look as if it is held up by anything. It looks as if it is held up by the light. There are forty windows in a ring round the foot of the dome, and the sun comes in through them in a great slow fall of gold, straight down, in shafts that you could lean on, and the whole interior is lit like the inside of a bell.
You stand in it. You are, you think, probably the only one of your kind in all the world who ever will. It does not hurt. It is the thing that you notice, and cannot help noticing: it does not hurt in the least. It is only very, very bright.
Somebody beside you, a fat old senator, in a splendid robe, says to his neighbour in a loud whisper that the Emperor has been heard to say that he has outdone the builder of the temple at Jerusalem. The neighbour says that it is a thing that he ought not to say. The senator says that it is a thing that he is, nevertheless, quite right about.
The Emperor comes in. He is a middle-sized grey man in purple, with a heavy, smooth, tired face, and he walks very slowly, and stops, once, in the middle of the nave, and looks up, for a long time, at the dome. You watch his face. It is not the face of a man who is pleased. It is the face of a man who has built the thing he wanted and is beginning to wonder what to want next.
You have, in the light, a few hours. You could do with them what you like.
* Listen to the court, and remember it all, for the Chamberlain
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ standing += 1
  You stand at a pillar and listen. It is the single most informative afternoon of your life. They speak Greek, and Latin, and bad Latin, in the low voices of people who are quite sure that they are not being overheard. You hear who is out of favour, and who is in, and who is owed money, and who is sleeping with whom, and what the Empress thinks of the Patriarch, and how the Emperor is going to pay for the next war. You do not write it down. You do not need to.
  It is, you suppose, a very useful thing to have.
* Sell the court a little oil, and a few good loans
  $ do earn(12)
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  You have, in the course of the afternoon, a number of very agreeable conversations. It is a great deal easier than you had expected. The court is very rich, and in a very good mood, and full of the sort of people who are on the point of being in debt. You come out of the Great Church, at dusk, with a great many promises, and a very full notebook, and a clear conscience, as far as it goes.
* Stand in it, and do nothing at all
  $ humanity += 3
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  You stand under the dome, in the light, until it goes. It is a thing that you will never be able to explain. It is the first time in three hundred years that you have stood in a room that was made to hold the sun, and it does not ask you for anything, and you stay.
~> evening
-> settle

# evening
## Dusk
It is dusk before the crowd thins. The long gold shafts of the light go dim, and amber, and red, and go up the walls like a tide going out, and the lamps are lit, and the great church becomes a place of shadows, and soft light, and the low murmur of a great many people saying goodbye.
You come out on the steps in the last of the sun. The litters are gone from the western wall. There is a single closed one left, at the foot of the steps, with a Lictor beside it. The curtain is a hand's breadth open.
?if lc.answer == "defiant" and lc.sunwalk == "refused"
  $ lc.court_537 = "hostile"
  There is nobody in the gap. It is, you think, a very long time since anybody has spoken your name with such a complete lack of affection.
?else
  A hand comes out of the gap. It is a small, plump, steady one, with a plain ring. It holds a piece of vellum, folded small. You take it. *Thank you,* it says, in the small clear hand, and nothing more.
  ?if lc.answer == "humble" or (lc.sunwalk == "shown" and lc.answer != "defiant")
    $ lc.court_537 = "allied"
  ?else
    $ lc.court_537 = "wary"
  ?end
?end
It is a short walk to the harbour. You do not hurry. You have, for the first time in a good while, nowhere that you need to be, and nobody who is waiting.
<~

# settle
## The third lying down
It is the last of it. You have a ship to find, and a hill to see to, and a thousand years to cover, in the dark, in a very short time.
You have done it twice. It is not a thing that gets easier. It is a thing that gets quicker.
~> place
~> steward
~> orders
~> guard
-> bed

# place
## Where you will lie
* The cellar on the hill, with the bell and the Sleeper's chapel overhead
  $ do rest_place("collesanto")
  $ bond.aelia += 1
  It is, it appears, a place of pilgrimage. It is the only thing to be said for it. It is, you think, probably the safest house in Italy, as long as the hill holds, and as long as nobody reads the inscription.
* A haven in a city, among people who could help
  $ do rest_place("haven")
  $ secrecy -= 1
  You buy a house in a quarter of the East that nobody in the West has heard of. It is the middle of a great busy place, among people who have never seen you, and it is the best hiding that there is.
* The tomb in the rock
  $ do rest_place("tomb")
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  It is cold. It is dry. It is very, very quiet. You tell yourself that it will be over quickly.
<~

# steward
## Who will hold what is yours
* The house of the Merchant line, as a bank and a trust
  $ do steward("bank")
  $ bond.prisca += 1
  The bank has done well. It has answered every letter. It has, as far as you know, never lied, and it has never, in five hundred years, once been grateful.
* Prisca, in her own person ?if in_circle("prisca") and member("prisca", "status") == "active"
  $ do steward("prisca")
  $ bond.prisca += 3
  $ standing += 1
  "I shall be exact," says Prisca. She has said it before. She says it, this time, with a very small sigh, as of a woman who knows that she has once again been made to give her word, and is going to keep it, and does not at all expect to be thanked.
* Hanno, who has kept the ledgers ?if in_circle("hanno") and member("hanno", "status") == "active"
  $ do steward("hanno")
  $ bond.hanno += 3
  He says nothing at all. He reads the paper, and signs, and bows, and goes out. It is, you gather, a great honour.
* Tiro, who is young and quick ?if in_circle("tiro") and member("tiro", "status") == "active"
  $ do steward("tiro")
  $ bond.tiro += 3
  Tiro says that he has done it once and will do it again, and that he would like it noted that he was brave. You note it. It is, by now, rather an understatement.
* The Valerii of the hill, the keepers ?if b1.keepers == "sworn"
  $ do steward("keepers")
  $ bond.aelia += 2
  $ bond.felix += 2
  Valerian reads the paper, slowly, with his lips moving. "It is a family," he says. "It will go on." He looks at the word *keepers* for some time. "It is a great deal. It is what a family is for."
<~

# orders
## What is to be done with it
* Play it safe: land, grain, honest rents
  $ do orders("safe")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is the choice for an age that has been robbed too often to want anything else.
* Grow it aggressively: shipping, loans, trade
  $ do orders("aggressive")
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It is a good time for it. The Emperor is rebuilding the world, and he has not got the money.
* The dark ventures, which pay the most, and ask nothing
  $ do orders("dark")
  $ cunning_brutal += 3
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ velati_regard -= 2
  It is the third time that you have written it. It does not get easier. It does, you find, get more convincing.
<~

# guard
## What stands between
:guard
* Lay traps in the ways in [once] ?if wealth >= 10
  $ do protection("traps")
  It is by now a perfectly routine matter.
* Pay the people who would otherwise ask questions [once] ?if wealth >= 20
  $ do protection("bribes")
  $ secrecy += 2
  It is, as usual, expensive.
* Hire guardians, sworn and paid [once] ?if wealth >= 30
  $ do protection("guardians")
  They are, you are told, the best that the East can offer. It is a thing that is said of a good many things.
* That will do
  <~
-> :guard

# bed
## Down again
$ do rest()
$ do sleep("byzantium_to_plague")
It is cold, wherever you are, and dark, and there is nothing at all to be heard. You lie down, and fold your hands, and let it take you. It is not at all like the first time. It is not like the second. It is like going to bed in a house where you have lived a very long time, and know all the creaks.
There is a sound as you go, far above you, a long way up, and a long way off. It is a bell. It is a very large one. It is ringing for something that you cannot hear. You think that it is the new church. You think that it is, perhaps, for a thousand years.
=> next
